  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 PM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nToday - Tonight: Heat Finally Comes to an End\n\nEarly this afternoon, heat indices across portions of NE Iowa and SW \nWisconsin have soared into the 100-115 degree range as 78-82+ degree \ndew points pool ahead of a weak cold front sliding southeast across \nthe region. Behind the front across SE Minnesota, dew points have \nstarted to drop into the lower 70s, holding heat indices between 90-\n102 degrees - still warm but slightly less oppressive. Slightly \nlower dew points will continue to filter in through the afternoon \nhours as the front makes its way through the entire area. Dew points \nthrough the overnight hours will drop into the upper 50s to 60s area \nwide, which should feel comparatively better than what we've \nbeen experiencing the last few days. \n\nTuesday - The Weekend: Slightly Less Humid, Rain Chances Late Week\n\nAs we move further into the work week, the upper level trough \ndominating the upper levels across the CONUS will start to \nretrograde to the west ever so slightly. This will allow slightly \ncooler northwest flow to prevail across the region. Weak surface \nhigh pressure is expected to build in behind the aforementioned weak \ncold front today, keeping conditions dry through Wednesday. \nTemperatures will remain on the warm side as temperatures aloft \ndon't see much of a cool down with surface highs in the mid to \nupper 80s both days. \n\nThe upper ridge will continue to slide west and intensify, allowing \na shortwave trough axis to rotate south and east out of the northern \nplains and into the Upper Midwest Great Lakes. This source of lift \ncombined with northward surging gulf moisture will allow for rain \nchances to return late Thursday into Friday. Soundings for this time \nperiod show little to no appreciable shear and weak instability, \nlending credence to the lack of risk areas across the Driftless\nRegion in any of the machine learning/CSU severe outlooks. The \nmain concern with these storms would be heavy rainfall as pWats \nlook to be approaching 2 inches, especially across Northeast \nIowa and SW Wisconsin. WPC has already included our area in a\nSlight Risk with their Day 5 Excessive Rainfall Outlook to \nhighlight this potential. Rain chances look to gradually come \nto an end through the day on Saturday with dry conditions \nexpected for the rest of the weekend. Temperatures Friday and \nSaturday will be slightly cooler given the widespread rain \nchances with highs in the upper 70s to low 80s.",
